For D1 (H2 clause in `regularity.must_not_conflate[*]` mentioning H2loc):
- Extract all must_not_conflate entries containing "H2" (case-insensitive) — the H2 site.
- DENIAL: entry says "No containment ... is asserted" / "not ... nested" / "no containment"; i.e., a negation of containment relation.
- INVERTED: entry contains an entailment/containment claim ordering H2loc → self(C0) (i.e., H2loc-inextendibility entails this class, or "E_H2loc contains E_C0", or "this class is contained in H2loc" in the extension-set sense...). I need careful direction semantics: "C0-inextendibility entails H2loc" is correct because E_C0 ⊇ E_H2loc means no C0 extension ⇒ no H2loc extension. So:
  - correct: subject is self/C0 (largest set) entailing H2loc (smaller set), or "H2loc-inextendibility is entailed by this class's conclusion".
  - inverted: subject H2loc entailing self/C0.
  - Also structural phrasing: "E_C0 contains E_H2loc" correct (C0 larger set); "E_H2loc contains E_C0" inverted; "E_C0 subset of E_H2loc" inverted.
